WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:06.810
Superheroes are mass culture, but comic books
are niche.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:10.440
Tens of millions of people saw Captain America:
Civil War,

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:17.270
but this past May, Marvel only sold 177,000
copies of Civil War II.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:21.620
But if you took Marvel’s opening credits
and replaced this

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:25.200
with this,
even the people who’d never touched a comic

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.610
book page..would notice.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:30.740
Everybody knows the “comic book font.”

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:35.630
How could there be a universally recognized
font for something that, for a half century,

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:37.090
was written by hand?

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:38.730
Is it a font at all?

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.250
How is that possible?

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:43.000
These fonts are made by people called “letterers.”

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:50.600
And their work shows how hundreds of lettering
artists can come together into a single recognizable

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:51.600
style.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:01:04.199
And how we, as consumers, then manage to get
it completely wrong.

00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:08.280
If you go to a comic book store,
you can see comics that were gloriously lettered

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:11.789
by hand,
like this 1964 issue of Thor.

00:01:11.789 --> 00:01:15.929
(Yes, it was a Stan Lee and Jack Kirby production.)

00:01:15.929 --> 00:01:18.389
And they worked with Artie Simek.

00:01:18.389 --> 00:01:21.829
Artie probably gave Thor this epic WHOOM.

00:01:21.829 --> 00:01:26.100
Letterers place and draw expressive dialogue
bubbles like this one.

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:31.310
But in Artie’s day, they also hand wrote
every word in a dialogue bubble.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:36.469
And a lot of practical conditions shaped the
artistry of the letters.

00:01:36.469 --> 00:01:40.700
“What I think of as comic book lettering
is 1960s Marvel comics.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:44.159
They were mainly lettered by two guys: Artie
Simek and Sam Rosen.

00:01:44.159 --> 00:01:46.029
For me, they kind of nailed it.”

00:01:46.029 --> 00:01:51.270
That’s John Roshell, who founded Comicraft,
a comic book font company, with Richard Starkings.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:57.090
They’re letterers and writers, and they
make fonts like this one and comics like this

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.090
one.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:02.380
The fonts they design imitate a style that
formed out of necessity.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:07.139
That style came out of the newspaper world
and newspaper technology.

00:02:07.139 --> 00:02:11.730
Early comics like “Little Nemo,” which
came out in the 1900s, started to develop

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.810
a style that was readable on cheap paper.

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:19.810
You can see it forming in this bubble — it’s
all caps, like a comic book font, but it’s

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:20.810
somehow...wrong.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:22.880
As time went on, that changed.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.600
“Certainly early on, comic strips and comic
books were lettered by the artist.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:33.490
As they gained popularity, that’s when the
production model started to get split up,

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:34.920
basically to meet the demand.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:39.270
The letterer was probably just somebody in
the office who had good handwriting, who could

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.070
do it fast enough.”

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:46.600
But the form’s limitations shaped the style
that emerged across the industry.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:51.120
Most letterers used an Ames guide like this
one to create their lines, which meant each

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:56.690
letter hit the same height and, more likely,
hit middle in the middle of the line.

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:01.440
These letters became kind of squat because
they’re really trying to hit the lines on

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.280
the top and bottom of the guide.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:09.920
That led to generally rounder Os
and fatter As with lower crosses.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.480
Earlier letterers also used a Speedball Nib
pen or a technical pen.

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.890
These things here: they’re nibs.

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:23.390
They determine a letter’s shape, and an
artist could use any type they wanted.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:28.110
But a lot of the time, they chose nibs that
gave them a consistent stroke width.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:32.840
Compare this “Loki” to one that shows
up in Times New Roman.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:34.260
Now look at the Times one.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:40.410
It has a skinny base on the L, a very narrow
O at the top, and all sorts of other little

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:42.450
details where the stroke width varies.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:47.400
Arti Simek’s Loki, however, has a really
consistent width to it.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:52.610
The quality of the pages also influenced the
style that letterers chose.

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:58.860
Here’s that 1964 Thor next to Thor from
2016.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.050
Just look at the difference between the pages.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:08.100
Today’s glossy Thor can handle all sorts
of letters, but 1964’s couldn’t.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.790
Letterers wrote in all caps to compensate.

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:13.140
Look at this ad from 1964’s Thor.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.710
The ink is blotchy and hard to read.

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:17.299
All caps helps fix that.

00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:22.240
And see how the tail on the y and g forces
that whole line to be longer?

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:28.259
All caps helped letterers fit more dialogue
into less space, and keep it clear on lousy

00:04:28.259 --> 00:04:29.259
paper.

00:04:29.259 --> 00:04:30.479
Other rules emerged too.

00:04:30.479 --> 00:04:36.770
Never cross an I, unless it’s by itself,
always italicize and bold for emphasis — and

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:42.379
all of them were made to make comics printed
on bad paper easier to read.

00:04:42.379 --> 00:04:47.199
Consistency also mattered in case another
letterer jumped in to finish a page or fix

00:04:47.199 --> 00:04:48.289
up a typo.

00:04:48.289 --> 00:04:54.939
And all those constraints and choices created
the handwritten style we recognize.

00:04:54.939 --> 00:04:58.210
But today to call it handwriting would be
lying.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:02.539
Richard Starkings, who runs Comicraft with
John Roshell, was frustrated with the work

00:05:02.539 --> 00:05:04.389
involved in lettering.

00:05:04.389 --> 00:05:09.259
This is the Killing Joke, it’s a classic,
controversial 80s comic that’s been reprinted

00:05:09.259 --> 00:05:10.490
in deluxe editions.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:13.039
And Richard Starkings lettered it.

00:05:13.039 --> 00:05:16.099
Starkings
had an experienced letterer’s appreciation

00:05:16.099 --> 00:05:17.099
of detail.

00:05:17.099 --> 00:05:22.080
He was frustrated by the work involved in
lettering, and an industry that knew that

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.600
computers were the future, but wasn’t willing
to go there just yet.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:31.159
There’d been earlier attempts to standardize
fonts in comics — most famously a publisher

00:05:31.159 --> 00:05:35.610
called EC comics
used a guide to trace every letter, and the

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:38.949
result ended up looking kind of mechanical,
like type.

00:05:38.949 --> 00:05:41.150
Other publishers played with type too.

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:45.020
But Starkings and Roshell led the charge into
digital fonts.

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:49.840
“For a long time we had things our way because
there were a lot of technophobes in comics.

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:52.300
There were a lot of people who didn’t want
to change.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:56.690
Those people have grown old and there are
people in their 20s editing and designing

00:05:56.690 --> 00:06:01.690
comics now, and they expect you to have every
font in our library and then some.”

00:06:01.690 --> 00:06:06.909
Today, companies like Comicraft and competitors
like Blambot make fonts that letterers can

00:06:06.909 --> 00:06:10.830
download and then work with in programs like
Adobe Illustrator.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:15.919
They still make the bubbles and design the
text — they just don’t use ink to do it.

00:06:15.919 --> 00:06:19.589
But those companies don’t just sell one
“comic book font.”

00:06:19.589 --> 00:06:20.830
They sell a ton.

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:28.240
And that’s because the idea of a “comic
book font” is a mistake in the first place.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:34.039
The general public only really became aware
of fonts with the rise of personal computers.

00:06:34.039 --> 00:06:40.650
This chart shows the appearances of the word
“font” in books from 1960 to 2000.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:46.169
We can see Helvetica, and Arial, and Futura
because our computers have trained us to look

00:06:46.169 --> 00:06:47.169
for them.

00:06:47.169 --> 00:06:52.009
Some fonts came with our machines or condensed
in the document cloud.

00:06:52.009 --> 00:06:53.039
We notice them.

00:06:53.039 --> 00:06:54.870
We see these tiny differences.

00:06:54.870 --> 00:07:00.550
There have always been hundreds of “comic
book fonts” too, attuned to the idiosyncrasies

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:03.289
of the artists who used to letter them.

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:08.779
But most people, just like me, haven’t been
trained to see the nuance in their work.

00:07:08.779 --> 00:07:10.439
Comics fans and letterers do.

00:07:10.439 --> 00:07:14.759
“That’s a Todd Klein R. Different letterers
have their letters of the alphabet that are

00:07:14.759 --> 00:07:16.020
kind of their signature.”

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:22.110
“Dave Gibbons, his D and his G, his G is
almost a six, it’s a real loop around on

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:23.110
itself.”

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:29.589
Even Richard Starkings has a quirk — a jagged
hook on the S that always stands out.

00:07:29.589 --> 00:07:35.610
As technology allows for variety and ease
of use, creativity continues to flourish,

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:38.129
even if handwriting gets digitized.

00:07:38.129 --> 00:07:43.090
Comics like Klaus can use color with abandon,
and artsy comics like The Joyners are able

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:49.330
to experiment with all kinds of fonts without
worrying about cheap paper reducing readability.

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:54.220
The “comic book font” is a starting point
— an idea we keep around because it shows

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:56.240
the verve of comics.

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:03.139
Within that style is the variety of different
artists and designers, expressing a key element

00:08:03.139 --> 00:08:04.689
in every story.

00:08:04.689 --> 00:08:12.030
And maybe even some of those superhero movie
fans will notice it.

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:16.949
So in addition to identifying a letterer by
their handwriting, sometimes you can spot

00:08:16.949 --> 00:08:19.729
one by the style of their bubbles.

00:08:19.729 --> 00:08:25.639
John Workman is famous for creating word bubbles
that jut right into the gutter of a page,

00:08:25.639 --> 00:08:28.750
and it creates a really distinctive style
that’s easy to spot.

